<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt</h1><p class="st-meta">664 BCE – 525 BCE · capital Sais</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Saite Dynasty, which freed Egypt from Assyrian and Kushite control and gave it a last long era of native prosperity.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-i">Psamtik I</a>, son of the Saite ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-i">Necho I</a>, began in 664 BCE as an Assyrian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> drove out the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kushite-empire">Kushite Empire</a>. As Assyria weakened he threw off its overlordship, and in 656 BCE he took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> peacefully by having his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitocris-i">Nitocris</a> adopted as the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gods-wife-of-amun">God's Wife of Amun</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Saite kings relied on Greek and Carian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> and allowed Greek traders to settle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naucratis">Naukratis</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/necho-ii">Necho II</a> began a canal from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, sent a Phoenician fleet around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and killed King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josiah">Josiah</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Judah</a> at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/megiddo">Megiddo</a>, but lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-carchemish">Carchemish</a> in 605 BCE. Art and texts deliberately revived <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/old-kingdom-of-egypt">Old Kingdom</a> models, and the Demotic script came into use. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasis-ii">Amasis II</a> courted Greek cities.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 525 BCE the Persian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambyses-ii">Cambyses II</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/psamtik-iii">Psamtik III</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelusium">Pelusium</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/memphis-egypt">Memphis</a>, turning the country into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/achaemenid-egypt">Achaemenid Egypt</a>. Saite art and religion shaped Late Period <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and Greek knowledge of Egypt, through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a>, rests largely on this era.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt.jpg" alt="Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt" width="320" height="338"><figcaption>Image: Jona Lendering · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_a_Pharaoh_of_the_Saite_Dynasty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>664 BCE – 525 BCE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sais</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Psamtik I, Necho II, Amasis II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Egyptian religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Egyptian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Saite Dynasty, Saite Egypt, Twenty-sixth Dynasty</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-594&amp;focus=twenty-sixth-dynasty-of-egypt" aria-label="Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5476 1049 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Twenty-sixth Dynasty of Egypt lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5796 1305L